# accessioned 2026-09-14 at MDU. Drafted by the P5 pipeline; verbatims replaced by
# the page-image transcription (p. 99, image read by Claude the same day; the
# pipeline's OCR-body verbatims carried "systemj", "constraint¡", "(S, E)").
##############################################################################

entry:joslyn-1995-system
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §2.6, Definition 21 (System), p. 99"@en ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:joslyn-1995-semantic-control ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.6 The Synthetic View, Definition 21, p. 99 (page-image transcription section)""" ;
    atlas:verbatim """Thus we arrive at a synthetic definition of system in terms of distinctions, variety, and constraint.

Definition 21 (System) A system is a cardinal distinction on a variety of dimensional distinctions.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """A system is a cardinal distinction on a variety of dimensional distinctions.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """Thus we arrive at a synthetic definition of system in terms of distinctions, variety, and constraint.""" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:cardinal-distinction ,
                           prim:dimensional-distinction ,
                           prim:variety ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim re-transcribed from the page image by claude-fable-5-1 2026-09-14 — not yet human-read" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY THIS IS THE GENUS ENTRY. The paper offers several system definitions: Definition 4 (p. 89, "my own natural language definition": an entity resulting from parts entering into relation, at a hierarchically distinct level), Definition 9 (p. 91, system₂: a bounded region of a space functionally and uniquely distinguished), and Definition 21, which the author introduces as the point he "arrives at" — "a synthetic definition of system in terms of distinctions, variety, and constraint." The catalogue takes the author's own word "synthetic" and the position of the definition (the close of §2, before control is introduced) as marking it the definition proper. Definitions 4 and 9 are include-graded candidates (scan candidates 3 and 6) still to be ruled on at the guided pass: one entry per distinct definitional claim may yet make them entries 014/015.

PARTS, NOT ENTRIES. Definitions 19 (cardinal distinction: "a constraint₂ which creates a system₁", p. 95) and 20 (dimensional distinction, p. 95) define the definition's two primitives and enter the scheme with those sentences.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about. CASES: none at this locus."""@en .

entry:joslyn-1995-contingent-metasystem
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §2.7.1, Definition 22 (Contingent Metasystem), p. 99"@en ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:joslyn-1995-semantic-control ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.7.1 Contingent Metasystems, Definition 22, p. 99 (page-image transcription section)""" ;
    atlas:verbatim """Definition 22 (Contingent Metasystem) Given a system₁ S with environment E (where E is understood to be similar to the constraint₁ C), then let
S′ := ⟨S, E⟩
be the metasystem, with parts S and E, generated contingently on S.

This point is driven home particularly in the case of simple distinctions, but it also holds in general. In the creation of any system₁ S, another metasystem S′ is identifiable contingent on considering S and its environment together as new, distinct entities.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """Given a system₁ S with environment E (where E is understood to be similar to the constraint₁ C), then let
S′ := ⟨S, E⟩
be the metasystem, with parts S and E, generated contingently on S.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """In the creation of any system₁ S, another metasystem S′ is identifiable contingent on considering S and its environment together as new, distinct entities.""" ;
    atlas:authorCaveat """(where E is understood to be similar to the constraint₁ C)""" ;
    atlas:speciesOf entry:joslyn-1995-system ;
    atlas:kindRestriction "metasystem: the pair ⟨S, E⟩ of a system₁ and its environment, generated contingently on S; genus word in the passage: system₁ — the catalogue points at Definition 21, the paper's synthetic definition proper, as the genus entry" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:system-1 ,
                           prim:environment ,
                           prim:constraint-1 ,
                           prim:part ,
                           prim:generated-contingently-on ,
                           prim:metasystem ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim re-transcribed from the page image by claude-fable-5-1 2026-09-14 — not yet human-read" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY IT IS AN ENTRY AND A SPECIES. A metasystem is a system (it has parts S and E) of a stated kind: formed from a system and its environment. It is the form Definition 25 later takes as given ("Given a metasystem CS := ⟨C, O⟩"), so it is the genus of the control entries and a species of Definition 21. The species chain the catalogue records is 009 (control₂) → 013 (metasystem) → 012 (system).

GENUS WORD. The passage says system₁, the subscripted classical sense, not the synthetic "system" of Definition 21. The pointer to 012 is the catalogue's choice (Definition 21 is the definition proper the author arrives at); if Definition 4 or 9 is later accessioned as system₁ the pointer should move. Recorded so the choice is visible, not silent.

PRIMITIVES. environment, constraint-1 and part are reused (same words as existing entries); system-1 and metasystem are new. DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about. CASES: none at this locus."""@en .
